---
import { repoData } from '../lib/repoData';
const { ruleCount, domainCount, contributorCount } = repoData;
---

<section class="sec-rule" id="why">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="story-grid">
      <div class="story-copy">
        <div class="kicker">Why OpenShield exists</div>
        <h2 class="story-h">Enterprise cloud security costs $50,000 to $500,000 a year. Everyone else gets zero visibility.</h2>
        <p class="story-p">Wiz, Prisma Cloud and Microsoft Defender for Cloud price out startups, SMEs, universities and student teams. A misconfigured storage blob, an overprivileged service principal or an open NSG rule can sit undetected for months. OpenShield closes that gap with an open source scanner anyone can run, read and extend.</p>
        <blockquote class="story-quote">Built by security engineers and students who believe cloud security tooling should be accessible to everyone.</blockquote>
        <div class="story-proof">FREE FOREVER / MIT LICENCE / OPENSSF PASSING / {contributorCount} CONTRIBUTORS</div>
      </div>
      <div class="story-cards">
        <div class="story-card sc-gap"><span class="tag">The gap</span><h3>Priced out of posture</h3><p>Security posture management is sold as an enterprise licence. Teams without budget inherit blind spots instead.</p></div>
        <div class="story-card sc-threat"><span class="tag">The threat</span><h3>Harvest now, decrypt later</h3><p>Adversaries record encrypted Azure traffic today to break it with tomorrow's quantum machines. RSA and ECC assets need migration before that day arrives.</p></div>
        <div class="story-card sc-answer"><span class="tag">The answer</span><h3>Open rules, real fixes</h3><p>{ruleCount} auditable Python rules across {domainCount} Azure domains, each with a remediation script and CIS, NIST, ISO 27001 and SOC 2 mapping. MIT licensed, free forever.</p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
